This looks like a SWE-bench-style task (Multi-SWE-bench Java) built on jackson-databind issue #4400-ish. The fix is small: in `createArrayDeserializer`, apply the modifiers to the primitive array deserializer as well.

The current code is:

```java
if (contentDeser == null) {
    Class<?> raw = contentType.getRawClass();
    if (contentType.isPrimitive()) {
        return PrimitiveArrayDeserializers.forType(raw);
    } else if (raw == String.class) {
        return StringArrayDeserializer.instance;
    }
}
```

The fix assigns the result to `deser` and then falls through to the modifier loop. The agent must restructure this properly. It is also possible that the hidden test checks something specific, such as whether the modifier is called for `byte[]`. A straightforward fix should satisfy that.

A complication is that the String array case also returns directly. The actual fix may have handled both, and the test might cover String[] too. The upstream fix (2.18, #4402) did:

```java
if (contentDeser == null) {
    if (contentType.isPrimitive()) {
        deser = PrimitiveArrayDeserializers.forType(raw);
    }
}
```

and then, for String, `deser = StringArrayDeserializer.instance`. Most agents would read the issue and do the primitive case, and probably the String case too once they see the code. The task looks moderately easy, and MiniMax M2.5 is a decent model, so I estimate a fairly high chance of success.

