This is a SWE-bench-style task (Multi-SWE-bench or SWE-bench Java) on jackson-databind, fixing the interaction between ALLOW_COERCION_OF_SCALARS and int-to-float coercion.

The actual fix lives in `StdDeserializer._checkIntToFloatCoercion` or similar. It special-cases the check so that when the target type is float and the input is an integer, `ALLOW_COERCION_OF_SCALARS` being disabled does not cause failure. The change is in `CoercionConfigs.findCoercion`: before the `ALLOW_COERCION_OF_SCALARS` check, it adds a condition for `inputShape == Integer && targetType == Float`. The fix is small, but it requires understanding the code. Under the old logic, `config.isEnabled(ALLOW_COERCION_OF_SCALARS)` returned fail when false.

The fix in 2.14 `CoercionConfigs`:

```java
if (inputShape == CoercionInputShape.Integer) {
    if (targetType == LogicalType.Float) {
        return CoercionAction.TryConvert;
    }
}
```

This is placed before the `!_mapperFeature ALLOW_COERCION_OF_SCALARS` check. The change is plausible for a strong model to find. Hidden tests are likely the one in the PR, and a model could reasonably produce a fix that passes them. GPT-5.2 is strong, so I estimate a fairly high probability of success.

