This looks like a SWE-bench-style task from jackson-core (the TextBuffer inconsistency issue). The upstream fix made `resetWithEmpty` set `_currentSegment` to `NO_CHARS`, so `getTextBuffer` returns an empty array instead of null. A fix this small should be within reach, though the hidden test needs the exact behavior.

The actual fix in jackson-core 2.4.x/2.5: in `resetWithEmpty()`, `_inputStart = -1; _currentSize = 0; _inputLen = 0; _inputBuffer = null; _resultString = null; _resultArray = null; if (_hasSegments) clearSegments();`. The fix changed `getTextBuffer()`:

```java
if (_inputStart >= 0) return _inputBuffer;
if (_resultArray != null) return _resultArray;
if (_resultString != null) return (_resultArray = _resultString.toCharArray());
if (!_hasSegments) return _currentSegment;
return contentsAsArray();
```

With that, `_currentSegment` may be null after `resetWithEmpty`. The fix probably made `getTextBuffer` return `NO_CHARS` when `_currentSegment` is null. The test likely asserts a non-null, empty array both before and after `contentsAsString`.

The agent has to infer that expected behavior from an ambiguous issue, since the issue says it is unsure which value is expected. Hidden tests could check either null or empty, but empty is the more likely choice. Since the issue says "returns [] after", an agent choosing [] would probably pass.

I put the chance of success at about 0.7.

